{"id":"74b9142b-e77b-43ba-97fd-8db6d9c0087b","object":"chat.completion","created":1784988203,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # Validate input\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must be a dict\\\")\\n    if 'lengths' not in case or 'scores' not in case or 'budget' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required fields: lengths, scores, budget\\\")\\n    lengths = case['lengths']\\n    scores = case['scores']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    if not isinstance(lengths, list) or not isinstance(scores, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"lengths and scores must be lists\\\")\\n    if len(lengths) != len(scores):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"lengths and scores must have the same length\\\")\\n    n = len(lengths)\\n    for l in lengths:\\n        if not isinstance(l, int) or l < 0:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Each length must be a non-negative integer\\\")\\n    for s in scores:\\n        if not isinstance(s, (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Each score must be a number\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(budget, int) or budget < 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"budget must be a non-negative integer\\\")\\n\\n    # dp[c] = {'score': float, 'tokens': int, 'indices': list}\\n    dp = [None] * (budget + 1)\\n    dp[0] = {'score': 0.0, 'tokens': 0, 'indices': []}\\n\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        l = lengths[i]\\n        s = scores[i]\\n        # Skip zero-length items; they will be handled separately at the end\\n        if l == 0:\\n            continue\\n        # Process capacities in descending order to avoid using the same item twice\\n        for c in range(budget, l - 1, -1):\\n            prev = dp[c - l]\\n            if prev is None:\\n                continue\\n            new_score = prev['score'] + s\\n            new_tokens = prev['tokens'] + l\\n            new_indices = prev['indices'] + [i]\\n            cur = dp[c]\\n            if cur is None:\\n                dp[c] = {'score': new_score, 'tokens': new_tokens, 'indices': new_indices}\\n            else:\\n                # Tie-breaking: higher score, then fewer tokens, then lexicographically smaller indices\\n                if new_score > cur['score']:\\n                    dp[c] = {'score': new_score, 'tokens': new_tokens, 'indices': new_indices}\\n                elif new_score == cur['score']:\\n                    if new_tokens < cur['tokens']:\\n                        dp[c] = {'score': new_score, 'tokens': new_tokens, 'indices': new_indices}\\n                    elif new_tokens == cur['tokens']:\\n                        if new_indices < cur['indices']:\\n                            dp[c] = {'score': new_score, 'tokens': new_tokens, 'indices': new_indices}\\n\\n    # Find the best solution across all capacities (0..budget)\\n    best = None\\n    for c in range(budget + 1):\\n        cur = dp[c]\\n        if cur is None:\\n            continue\\n        if best is None:\\n            best = (cur['score'], cur['tokens'], cur['indices'])\\n        else:\\n            if cur['score'] > best[0]:\\n                best = (cur['score'], cur['tokens'], cur['indices'])\\n            elif cur['score'] == best[0]:\\n                if cur['tokens'] < best[1]:\\n                    best = (cur['score'], cur['tokens'], cur['indices'])\\n                elif cur['tokens'] == best[1]:\\n                    if cur['indices'] < best[2]:\\n                        best = (cur['score'], cur['tokens'], cur['indices'])\\n\\n    # Handle zero-length items (include them unconditionally)\\n    zero_indices = [i for i, l in enumerate(lengths) if l == 0]\\n    if best is None:\\n        # Only possible if budget < 0, but we already validated budget >= 0\\n        total_score = sum(scores[i] for i in zero_indices)\\n        selected_indices = zero_indices\\n        tokens_used = 0\\n    else:\\n        total_score = best[0] + sum(scores[i] for i in zero_indices)\\n        selected_indices = sorted(best[2] + zero_indices)  # keep ascending order\\n        tokens_used = best[1]  # zero-length items add no tokens\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"selected_indices\\\": selected_indices,\\n        \\\"tokens_used\\\": tokens_used,\\n        \\\"total_score\\\": total_score\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function solve(case) that implements a budgeted sentence selection problem. The input case has: lengths (list of ints), scores (list of floats), budget (int). There might be additional fields like _case_nonce, but we ignore. We need to select a subset of indices (0-indexed?) to maximize total score, without exceeding total length budget. Sentences must be selected in document order? The description says \"preserve document order\" and \"document_order\" contract. So we cannot reorder; selection is a subset preserving original order. Also ties: break equal utility by fewer used tokens, then lexicographic source indices (i.e., lower indices? Actually \"lexicographic source indices\" probably means if total score and total tokens are equal, choose the one with smaller indices? Possibly meaning choose the set with smaller lexicographic order of indices? But typical tie-breaking: if two subsets have same total score and same total tokens, pick the one with lexicographically smaller list of indices (i.e., earlier indices preferred). But careful: The description: \"Break equal utility by fewer used tokens and then lexicographic source indices.\" So first tie-breaker: fewer used tokens. Second tie-breaker: lexicographic source indices (i.e., compare the sorted list of selected indices? Or the original order? Usually lexicographic order of the sorted list, meaning the subset that has smaller first differing index, i.e., prefer lower indices). Since we preserve document order, the indices are naturally sorted. So we need to implement a DP that returns: selected_indices (list of ints, 0-indexed), tokens_used (sum of lengths of selected sentences), total_score (sum of scores). Ensure it's maximal.\n\nWe need to handle infeasibility: if no selection possible (e.g., budget < min length) => raise ValueError. But also if budget is 0? Possibly zero budget: no sentences selected, sum score 0, tokens_used 0, indices empty. That should be feasible. However, note that the description says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case\". Structurally invalid: missing fields or wrong types. We should validate that lengths and scores are lists, same length, all positive lengths? Actually lengths should be positive integers (tokens). Scores can be floats. Budget non-negative integer. We'll validate.\n\nImplement DP: Classic 0-1 knapsack with item count up to maybe 100? Unknown but standard DP O(N*budget). Since budget can be up to maybe large? But constraints not given; we use DP with list of dictionaries or array. Since we need to break ties and preserve decision, we can store for each capacity the best (score, tokens_used, last_index, previous decision) or use DP with tuple (score, tokens_used, indices). But we also need to reconstruct. We can use DP array of dictionaries: dp[c] = (best_score, best_tokens_used, best_indices) but that's heavy. Better: store for each capacity the best (score, tokens_used) and also a predecessor chain to reconstruct indices. But tie-breaking: when two candidates have same score and same tokens_used, we need to pick lexicographically smaller set of indices. That can be handled by comparing the list of indices. However, if we store predecessor, we can reconstruct the list and compare lexicographically. But that might be inefficient. We can incorporate tie-breaking into DP update: if new_score > dp[c].score, update; if equal and new_tokens < dp[c].tokens, update; if equal tokens and new_indices list is lexicographically smaller than dp[c].indices, update. Since we need to compare indices, we need to store the actual list or a hash. But list comparison is O(k) where k is number of items, which could be large. However, N is probably small (maybe up to 100) and budget up to maybe 1000, so it's okay.\n\nAlternatively, we can encode the selection as a bitmask if N <= 64, but N unknown. Safer to store the list.\n\nImplementation plan:\n- Validate case: must have 'lengths', 'scores', 'budget' keys. lengths and scores must be lists of equal length, lengths all int >= 0? Actually lengths should be positive? Maybe 0 length tokens? Unlikely. We'll allow 0? But if length 0, it's free. That could be problematic. We'll assume positive integers. Budget non-negative int.\n- N = len(lengths)\n- If budget < 0: raise ValueError.\n- Initialize DP as array of size budget+1, each element is a dict with keys: score, tokens_used, indices (list). For c=0, score=0, tokens_used=0, indices=[].\n- For each item i from 0 to N-1 (in order), for capacity from budget down to len_i (if len_i <= budget), consider taking item i: new_score = dp[c - len_i].score + scores[i]; new_tokens = dp[c - len_i].tokens_used + len_i; new_indices = dp[c - len_i].indices + [i] (copy list). Then compare with current dp[c]:\n   - if new_score > dp[c].score: update.\n   - elif new_score == dp[c].score and new_tokens < dp[c].tokens_used: update.\n   - elif new_score == dp[c].score and new_tokens == dp[c].tokens_used and new_indices < dp[c].indices (lexicographically): update. Note: new_indices and dp[c].indices are both sorted because we add in order, so lexicographic compare works.\n- After processing all items, the best selection is dp[budget] (if we only care about exactly budget? Actually the problem says \"under an exact token budget\"? It says \"under an exact token budget\" but also \"never exceed the budget\". So we can use any total tokens up to budget? The typical knapsack: maximize sum of scores subject to total length <= budget. The statement: \"Select and reconstruct context-scored sentences under an exact token budget while preserving document order.\" That suggests we must exactly meet the budget? But it also says \"never exceed the budget\". Usually \"exact token budget\" means we cannot exceed, but we can use less. However, the contract \"budget_feasible\" likely means we need to check if there is any selection that does not exceed. So we allow <= budget. The DP normally considers all capacities up to budget; we take the best among all capacities <= budget? But the tie-breaking may prefer fewer tokens, so the best over all capacities might be with fewer tokens if same score. However, the DP only stores best for each capacity; we need to find the best overall among capacities 0..budget. Since we store per capacity, we can iterate all capacities and pick the best based on (score, -tokens? Actually tie-breaking: first score, then fewer tokens, then lexicographic indices. So we can compare across capacities.\nThus after DP, we compute best over all capacities c in 0..budget: track best_score, best_tokens, best_indices. For equal score, prefer smaller tokens; if equal tokens, lexicographically smaller indices.\n- If no selection found? But there is always empty selection (score 0, tokens 0). So feasible as long as budget >=0. However, the problem might require at least one sentence? The contract didn't say. But the return should include selected_indices, can be empty. That's fine.\n\nBut note: The DP as described uses O(N*budget) space if we store full indices for each capacity. We can optimize by storing predecessor info: for each capacity, store the last index taken and the previous capacity. Then reconstruct indices. But tie-breaking would require comparing the whole list. However, we can store the tuple (score, tokens_used, last_index, prev_capacity) and during reconstruction, we can compare lexicographically. But comparing two reconstructions might be expensive. Given typical small sizes, we can store indices list.\n\nWe'll implement with list of indices.\n\nEdge cases: lengths may be 0? If so, we can always include it for free. That could cause issues with DP because we would skip zero-length items? We should handle: if length_i == 0, we can always include it without consuming budget. But then the selection would include it automatically? Since it doesn't cost, the optimal would include all zero-length items to increase score. But the problem might not have zero-length. We'll ignore and assume positive.\n\nAlso ensure we don't mutate case.\n\nReturn dict with selected_indices, tokens_used (sum of lengths), total_score (sum of scores). Use int for tokens_used, float for total_score.\n\nNow write the solve function.\n\nNote: The output must be JSON-serializable. Python's list and dict are fine. Use math.\n\nImplementation in Python:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"case must be a dict\")\n    if 'lengths' not in case or 'scores' not in case or 'budget' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"missing required fields\")\n    lengths = case['lengths']\n    scores = case['scores']\n    budget = case['budget']\n    if not isinstance(lengths, list) or not isinstance(scores, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"lengths and scores must be lists\")\n    if len(lengths) != len(scores):\n        raise ValueError(\"lengths and scores must have same length\")\n    if not all(isinstance(l, int) and l >= 0 for l in lengths):\n        raise ValueError(\"lengths must be non-negative integers\")\n    if not all(isinstance(s, (int, float)) for s in scores):\n        raise ValueError(\"scores must be numbers\")\n    if not isinstance(budget, int) or budget < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"budget must be non-negative integer\")\n    n = len(lengths)\n    # dp[c] = {'score': float, 'tokens': int, 'indices': list}\n    dp = [None] * (budget + 1)\n    dp[0] = {'score': 0.0, 'tokens': 0, 'indices': []}\n    for i in range(n):\n        l = lengths[i]\n        s = scores[i]\n        if l == 0:\n            # auto include? Since free, we always want to include it to increase score.\n            # But must we? The DP would normally allow taking it without cost. However, if we update dp in place, we need to handle carefully.\n            # We could pre-include zero-length items by updating all dp entries.\n            # Better: treat separately. We'll include them unconditionally because they don't consume budget and only increase score.\n            # But tie-breaking: if two zero-length items with same score? We'll include all.\n            # However, note that the problem likely doesn't have zero-length. We'll skip for simplicity.\n            pass\n        # we need to iterate capacities in reverse to avoid using same item multiple times\n        for c in range(budget, l-1, -1):\n            prev = dp[c - l]\n            if prev is None:\n                continue\n            new_score = prev['score'] + s\n            new_tokens = prev['tokens'] + l\n            new_indices = prev['indices'] + [i]  # creates new list\n            current = dp[c]\n            if current is None:\n                dp[c] = {'score': new_score, 'tokens': new_tokens, 'indices': new_indices}\n            else:\n                # compare\n                if new_score > current['score']:\n                    dp[c] = {'score': new_score, 'tokens': new_tokens, 'indices': new_indices}\n                elif new_score == current['score']:\n                    if new_tokens < current['tokens']:\n                        dp[c] = {'score': new_score, 'tokens': new_tokens, 'indices': new_indices}\n                    elif new_tokens == current['tokens']:\n                        if new_indices < current['indices']:  # lexicographic\n                            dp[c] = {'score': new_score, 'tokens': new_tokens, 'indices': new_indices}\n    # find best among all capacities\n    best = None\n    for c in range(budget + 1):\n        cur = dp[c]\n        if cur is None:\n            continue\n        if best is None:\n            best = (cur['score'], cur['tokens'], cur['indices'])\n        else:\n            if cur['score'] > best[0]:\n                best = (cur['score'], cur['tokens'], cur['indices'])\n            elif cur['score'] == best[0]:\n                if cur['tokens'] < best[1]:\n                    best = (cur['score'], cur['tokens'], cur['indices'])\n                elif cur['tokens'] == best[1]:\n                    if cur['indices'] < best[2]:\n                        best = (cur['score'], cur['tokens'], cur['indices'])\n    if best is None:\n        # this should not happen because dp[0] exists\n        return {\"selected_indices\": [], \"tokens_used\": 0, \"total_score\": 0.0}\n    total_score = best[0]\n    tokens_used = best[1]\n    selected_indices = best[2]\n    # note: tokens_used should match sum(lengths[i] for i in selected_indices)\n    # total_score should match sum(scores[i] for i in selected_indices)\n    return {\"selected_indices\": selected_indices, \"tokens_used\": tokens_used, \"total_score\": total_score}\n\nBut we need to handle zero-length items properly. Let's verify: If there is a zero-length item, it should be included automatically because it adds score with no cost. The DP as written: for l=0, the loop for c in range(budget, -1, -1) (since l-1 = -1, range stops at -1? Actually range(budget, -1, -1) would include 0. But l=0, so condition c >= l is always true. However, the DP update would allow taking the same zero-length item multiple times if we iterate descending? Actually for 0-length, c - l = c, so it would be using dp[c] to update dp[c] itself, leading to infinite loop? In the descending loop, for each c, we read dp[c] (since c-l = c) which may have been updated earlier in the same iteration (since descending, c goes from budget down to 0, but when we update dp[c], later for smaller c we might read the updated dp[c]? Actually for each i, we process capacities descending. For l=0, the update would be: new_score = dp[c].score + s, new_indices = dp[c].indices + [i]. But dp[c] might already contain the best for this item? This could lead to multiple inclusions. To avoid this, we should skip zero-length items from the loop and handle them separately. Since the problem likely has positive lengths, we can assume lengths > 0. But to be safe, we can add a check: if l == 0: continue; then after DP, we can pre-append all zero-length items to the best selection? But those items would increase score and tokens_used? They don't increase tokens. So we should include all zero-length items unconditionally. So we can compute: zero_indices = [i for i, l in enumerate(lengths) if l == 0]. Then for each such index, we add its score to total_score and append to selected_indices. But careful: what if zero-length items have negative scores? Unlikely. We'll assume non-negative. For safety, we can include them anyway because they don't cost budget. But the problem didn't specify negative scores; probably positive. For simplicity, we'll ignore zero-length cases.\n\nAlso note: The DP uses list concatenation which creates new lists each time, could be heavy for large N and budget. We can optimize by storing predecessor indices and reconstructing later. But for now, given the problem size, it's acceptable.\n\nWe must ensure that the function is pure and uses only standard library. No imports needed.\n\nNow write the implementation as a string and embed in JSON.\n\nWe need to output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a JSON string containing the Python source.\n\nWe'll produce the code.\n\nPotential improvements: Use list of tuples for dp to avoid dictionaries? But dictionary is fine.\n\nAlso note: The tie-breaking \"lexicographic source indices\" means compare the list of selected indices as sorted? Since we add indices in order, the list is sorted ascending. So direct list comparison works.\n\nEdge case: If there are multiple ways to achieve same score and tokens, we prefer lexicographically smaller list. Our DP update ensures that when comparing new_indices < current['indices'], we update if new is smaller. Since we process items in order, for a given capacity, the first time we fill it might have a certain set; later alternatives might have larger indices (since we add higher i). So this tie-breaking will prefer earlier indices? Actually, if two subsets have same score and tokens, the one with smaller first differing index is lexicographically smaller. Since we add indices in increasing order, the list is sorted. The DP's update condition will prefer the smaller list. Since we process items i from 0 to n-1, the first time we update a capacity with a certain set, later updates for same capacity with same score and tokens might have larger indices (since later items added). But note: the order of adding items: when we consider item i, we update dp[c] by combining dp[c-l] (which has indices all less than i) + [i]. So the new list's last element is i, and previous indices are from dp[c-l] which are all < i. So when comparing two lists for same capacity, they will both end with some i and i' (maybe different). The lexicographic comparison will look at the first difference. Since the lists are sorted ascending, the one with smaller first element will be smaller. Typically, earlier indices lead to smaller first element. So the DP's update condition will keep the smaller list, which aligns with tie-breaking.\n\nOne nuance: For the same capacity, we might have two alternatives that achieve same score and tokens, one using a small index and another using a larger index but with different composition. The first one discovered might not be the lexicographically smallest if later updates produce a smaller list. But we update only if new_indices < current['indices'], so we replace when we find a smaller list. Since we process items in order, and the second alternative might come from a later item, its list